408→                           Erd&#337;s says this question was asked 'during [his] lecture at Queens College [by] one member of the audience (perhaps S. Shapiro)'. Such a sequence of $a_i$ is sometimes called a set of Beurling prime numbers (and the sequence of products called the associated generalised integers).<br><br>Beurling conjectured that if the number of reals in $[1,x]$ of the form $\prod a_i^{k_i}$ is $x+o(\log x)$ then the $a_i$ must be the sequence of primes.<br><br>It is unclear whether this is intended to hold for all $x$ or just all sufficiently large $x$. The former question can be disproved by finite calculation (since any finite squence of $a_i$ can be extended to a suitable infinite sequence via a greedy algorithm). A finite counterexample for $x=10$ was found by ChatGPT-5.2 Pro (prompted by Leeham).
